WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:18.433
It is my pleasure to introduce

00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:27.700
Verity Price, DTM.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.833
Verity Price

00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:32.566
is someone who believes in
chasing your dreams.

00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:39.100
She's gone from walking
750 miles across Europe in hiking shoes

00:00:39.166 --> 00:00:44.000
in memory of her parents
to performing her original songs

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
in high heels in front of stages

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.200
that are packed in Nashville and New York.

00:00:50.266 --> 00:00:54.466
From acting in what she admits might be

00:00:54.566 --> 00:00:57.366
B-level movie films

00:00:57.366 --> 00:01:03.500
with Steven Seagal and Sean Astin
to speaking on two TEDx stages.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.066
She went from single at 40

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:10.500
to happily married at 43,
and became a mother

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.600
to a beautiful baby boy long after

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.600
doctors said it was impossible.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.900
And then in lockdown,
she chased another dream

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.733
and in 2021, made history

00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:27.166
as the first speaker from Africa

00:01:27.266 --> 00:01:34.033
to be crowned
World Champion of Public Speaking.

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:34.500
With a

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.100
degree in psychology
from the University of Cape Town

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.400
and an Accredited DeBono Thinking
Facilitator, Verity

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.533
has presented to hundreds of companies
over the last 15 years.

00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:50.633
In 2022, she was invited to be

00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:56.400
a faculty member of the University of Cape
Town's Graduate School of Business,

00:01:56.466 --> 00:02:00.133
rated the top business school in Africa.

00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:03.800
Here,
she forms part of an executive education

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.800
department and delivers her content
as part of transformational work

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.600
they do with organizations
across the African continent.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.133
When it comes to making your dreams
a reality, Verity believes

00:02:18.133 --> 00:02:24.100
you need to act out of the box
more than think out of it.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.233
And now, all the way from South Africa

00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:33.100
to help us design our future and think

00:02:33.166 --> 00:02:35.300
and think ahead for the crowd,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:55.166
please welcome Verity Price.

00:02:55.233 --> 00:02:58.033
Thank you, Conference Chair.

00:02:58.033 --> 00:03:00.400
I've chased a lot of dreams in my life,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.866
but I have to tell you,
not in my wildest dreams did I ever

00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:09.333
imagine I would be on this stage
speaking to this audience

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.200
in The Bahamas.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.800
So am I safe to say
there's a lot of dreams in the room today?

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:18.766
Yeah.

00:03:18.766 --> 00:03:22.866
So if you've got big dreams, big hopes,
big aspirations, put up your hand.

00:03:22.866 --> 00:03:23.800
Put the ace in the chat.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.666
Put up two hands
if they're really big.

00:03:26.666 --> 00:03:28.933
Okay, thank you.

00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:30.933
Now, when you think about that dream

00:03:30.933 --> 00:03:33.966
or that goal, put up your other hand

00:03:33.966 --> 00:03:36.400
if you ever feel afraid, concerned.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.400
What if it doesn't work out?
What if I fail?

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.733
What if something goes wrong?

00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:42.633
Put up two hands.
If that feels really real.

00:03:42.633 --> 00:03:45.033
Do you know what this means?

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:47.733
You're human.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.933
Our dreams excite us and they terrify us.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:55.366
But in the 15 years that I've been working

00:03:55.366 --> 00:03:58.366
with hundreds of companies,
tens of thousands of people,

00:03:58.366 --> 00:04:02.533
helping them to change their thinking
so that they can reach their dreams,

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.600
I've come to see
what really makes the difference

00:04:05.833 --> 00:04:10.800
is if you are asking two questions
or giving one answer.

00:04:10.866 --> 00:04:13.233
Have I got any parents here today?

00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:15.233
Yeah. Some of you don't sound sure.

00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:18.700
Do you know? Are you good parents here?

00:04:18.766 --> 00:04:21.766
If you didn't say yes,
were you a child once?

00:04:21.866 --> 00:04:23.766
Okay, so you know this.

00:04:23.766 --> 00:04:27.000
When children learn to speak,
what is that irritating question

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.000
they ask all the time? Why? Mm.

00:04:31.066 --> 00:04:33.866
And then when they become teenagers,
they change it.

00:04:33.866 --> 00:04:36.366
They add another word. What do they say?

00:04:36.366 --> 00:04:37.300
Why not?

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.533
Okay. They start pushing back.

00:04:39.533 --> 00:04:42.533
I hear you and your rules,
and they push back.

00:04:42.766 --> 00:04:46.500
And so by the time we're adults,
until we die, we have a one word answer.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.300
What do we say?

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.600
Because. No. Because.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:52.666
Have you ever said that before?

00:04:52.666 --> 00:04:54.666
Because I said so.

00:04:54.666 --> 00:04:56.266
Because those are the rules.

00:04:56.266 --> 00:04:57.866
Because that's how it's done.

00:04:57.866 --> 00:05:00.133
Because it's safer that way.

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:05.400
And before you know it, because becomes
the box. It becomes a reaction to life

00:05:05.533 --> 00:05:08.533
where we survive, but we don't thrive.

00:05:08.666 --> 00:05:10.633
Are you here because you want to thrive?

00:05:10.633 --> 00:05:12.033
Yeah. Okay, good.

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:14.833
Has anyone ever told you
to think outside the box?

00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:15.566
Yeah.

00:05:15.566 --> 00:05:18.566
Well, I want you to consider that
all you need to think outside of

00:05:18.566 --> 00:05:22.333
is the because, all the reasons
it can't be done,

00:05:22.433 --> 00:05:24.666
and start asking,
"Why am I doing it this way?

00:05:24.666 --> 00:05:26.133
Why not do it differently?"

00:05:26.133 --> 00:05:29.766
and consider
all the reasons it can be done.

00:05:29.833 --> 00:05:34.000
Now, you might be wondering
why is Verity so passionate about this?

00:05:34.066 --> 00:05:37.066
I'm going to tell you. Twenty years ago

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.433
I was surviving the music industry.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.000
I was an aspiring singer and songwriter.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.166
I had big dreams.

00:05:44.166 --> 00:05:45.666
I wanted to tour the world.

00:05:45.666 --> 00:05:47.066
I wanted to top the charts.

00:05:47.066 --> 00:05:48.900
I wanted to be interviewed on Oprah.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.766
I didn't care what color her couch was.

00:05:53.833 --> 00:05:56.766
Yes. Yes.

00:05:56.766 --> 00:06:00.600
But two years into my career,
the only thing I was touring

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.600
was the bar at the end of my street.

00:06:04.666 --> 00:06:06.633
So on top of that, I then discovered

00:06:06.633 --> 00:06:11.733
it was going to cost me $40,000
to record my first album.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.333
That was a big pothole
between me and my dream.

00:06:16.433 --> 00:06:20.866
And my reaction to that
problem was to moan.

00:06:20.966 --> 00:06:22.833
I became one of those people

00:06:22.833 --> 00:06:26.033
who arrives at a party with their problem,

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.233
and then they find some poor person
and they spend the whole night

00:06:29.233 --> 00:06:32.833
telling them how hard and
how unfair their life is.

00:06:32.933 --> 00:06:35.633
Have you ever met someone like this?

00:06:35.633 --> 00:06:39.933
I hope they are not sitting in your seat.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.033
Eventually, I realized

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:46.233
I either needed to give up
or I needed to get creative.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.133
Which is the better choice?

00:06:48.133 --> 00:06:50.500
Get creative. Absolutely.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:56.533
So I got on the phone to my older,
much wiser sister, Kay.

00:06:56.633 --> 00:06:58.933
Now, Kay is the bookworm in our family.

00:06:58.933 --> 00:07:02.033
I asked her if we could have coffee
and she arrived and told me that she'd

00:07:02.033 --> 00:07:06.700
just finished reading a book called
the "Six Thinking Hats" by Edward de Bono.

00:07:06.766 --> 00:07:09.533
She was excited
to see if she could use his framework

00:07:09.533 --> 00:07:13.233
for problem solving to solve my problem.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.133
Now, I don't have time to take you
through his work today,

00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:20.166
but I want to take you through the
thinking we did that day.

00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:22.100
The first thing we realized was that

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.600
I was so focused on my problem,

00:07:25.666 --> 00:07:29.733
I hadn't looked up to focus on a solution.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:33.433
And if you have got obstacles between you
and where you want to be,

00:07:33.533 --> 00:07:37.000
it comes down to
are you asking extraordinary questions?

00:07:37.066 --> 00:07:41.466
Because extraordinary questions
get extraordinary solutions.

00:07:41.533 --> 00:07:42.000
And so my

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.133
extraordinary question that day was
how can I market

00:07:46.266 --> 00:07:51.100
and release an album in South Africa
without going into debt?

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.266
I put my problem into the question.

00:07:53.266 --> 00:07:57.300
I wanted to give my brain
a challenge to solve.

00:07:57.366 --> 00:08:01.133
Next, we stepped back and looked
at the facts of my current reality.

00:08:01.166 --> 00:08:01.833
What did I know?

00:08:01.833 --> 00:08:03.300
What did I need to know?

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.100
Now I knew that I didn't have $40,000.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.600
My bank balance agreed on that.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.500
But I also knew that I had 200 songs
written.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.333
I had a band. I had a website.

00:08:14.333 --> 00:08:16.566
I had a network of supportive people.

00:08:16.566 --> 00:08:20.066
Didn't know how you got music
into the stores, how you got music on

00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:21.266
the radio,

00:08:21.266 --> 00:08:24.700
but that mere
act of looking at the facts

00:08:24.766 --> 00:08:30.100
showed me things I could be doing
to move closer to my dream.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.066
Next, Kay said

00:08:31.066 --> 00:08:34.066
to me,
"Verity, how are you feeling about this?"

00:08:34.233 --> 00:08:37.033
But before I started, she said,

00:08:37.033 --> 00:08:38.566
"Name the feeling.

00:08:38.566 --> 00:08:40.266
Don't explain

00:08:40.266 --> 00:08:42.500
the feeling."

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:42.966
Okay.

00:08:42.966 --> 00:08:46.500
Well, I'm overwhelmed,
frustrated, anxious, irritated, scared,

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.033
tired. It was a very long list.

00:08:48.033 --> 00:08:51.333
I'd had a long time
to build up some feelings.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.600
But the amazing thing is,
is when you name a feeling

00:08:54.766 --> 00:08:56.700
but don't explain it,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.700
it just gets it out of your head,
puts it on the paper,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.233
and clears up your mind to keep thinking.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:07.100
Next, she said, "Verity,
what is working in your career?"

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.600
Now, this was embarrassing.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:13.133
In two years, I hadn't once looked at if
anything was working.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.166
But when I looked, I found it.

00:09:16.166 --> 00:09:17.100
I have songs written.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.700
I have a band. I have a website.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.300
There's less competition in South Africa.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:26.900
And when I focused on the positives,
I started feeling more positive.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:27.666
Then she said,

00:09:27.666 --> 00:09:30.866
"Okay, let's think how you have been
thinking for the last two years.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.800
What's not working?" This was easy.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:34.766
I don't have the money

00:09:34.766 --> 00:09:37.766
and because I don't have the money,
if I borrow it, I'm going to go into debt.

00:09:37.866 --> 00:09:40.733
Does this kind of thinking
sound familiar to you?

00:09:40.733 --> 00:09:41.733
Yeah. Okay.

00:09:41.733 --> 00:09:42.766
Isn't it interesting

00:09:42.766 --> 00:09:46.833
how easy it is to look at all
the reasons you can't do something?

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:48.666
But here's the beauty.

00:09:48.666 --> 00:09:52.366
If you look at the positives
before you look at the problems,

00:09:52.466 --> 00:09:56.566
it's much easier to then
consider possibilities.

00:09:56.666 --> 00:09:58.633
And so I suddenly looked at everything

00:09:58.633 --> 00:10:01.633
we'd written down and I went, "Hm.

00:10:01.733 --> 00:10:06.033
Just because I don't have $40,000,
it was a problem and a fact,

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.966
why not leverage off
the fact that my songs are written?

00:10:10.133 --> 00:10:12.066
I have a band. I have a website."

00:10:12.066 --> 00:10:15.800
And imagine, now you know 
you are accessing creative thinking

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:20.633
when you say things like "Imagine"
or "Wouldn't it be amazing if...?" And I went,

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:26.033
"Imagine if I asked everyone
I know to go onto my website

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:30.333
and buy copies of my album
before I record it.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.933
Imagine
if I sell something that doesn't exist

00:10:32.933 --> 00:10:35.933
and the people who buy it replace the record
label."

00:10:36.066 --> 00:10:38.433
Now Kay was an amazing thinking partner.
She said,

00:10:38.433 --> 00:10:41.233
"That's awesome. What else could you do?"

00:10:41.233 --> 00:10:45.300
Because here's the thing with thinking,
it is far easier for you

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:49.533
to tame a wild idea
than it is for you to make a boring idea

00:10:49.533 --> 00:10:51.433
interesting.

00:10:51.533 --> 00:10:52.766
Okay.

00:10:52.766 --> 00:10:55.766
So I said,
"All right, this is pretty different.

00:10:55.766 --> 00:10:57.766
Don't know if it's ever been done before."

00:10:57.766 --> 00:11:01.066
And I said, "Well,
maybe I can make a difference."

00:11:01.066 --> 00:11:05.866
And I decided that I would give 10% of
everything I raised to split it

00:11:05.866 --> 00:11:10.700
between women's shelters in South Africa
and empowering other artists.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.800
Two years I had been stuck.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:17.600
Thirty minutes of thinking differently
and I had a way forward.

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:20.100
I was going to sell an album
that didn't exist,

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:22.300
people
were going to replace a record label,

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.800
and we were going to give 10%
to empowering artists and uplifting women.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:29.066
Now you might be looking at this
and going, "Well,

00:11:29.066 --> 00:11:32.266
Verity,
isn't that just called crowdfunding?"

00:11:32.366 --> 00:11:34.366
And you'd be right.

00:11:34.366 --> 00:11:37.633
But do you know what
this idea was called in 2005?

00:11:37.733 --> 00:11:40.566
Crazy.

00:11:40.566 --> 00:11:42.200
I left that coffee

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:46.233
knowing I would be crazy not to try,

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:48.600
and I also left with a choice.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:53.133
Was I going to act on my idea or leave it
written on a napkin?

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.566
Because ideas have no value.

00:11:55.566 --> 00:11:58.566
Action has value.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.166
And if you are prepared
to flirt with failure,

00:12:01.166 --> 00:12:04.533
that's the only time you're ever going
to get to dance with success.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:08.533
And so the first action I took was
I started telling people about my idea.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:10.100
Two things happened.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.866
Everyone in the music industry
said, "That won't work

00:12:12.866 --> 00:12:16.500
because that's not how music is funded."

00:12:16.566 --> 00:12:19.033
Everyone who wasn't in the music industry
said, "Why not?

00:12:19.033 --> 00:12:19.733
That's crazy.

00:12:19.733 --> 00:12:24.033
Let's do it."
First person who said yes was a guy.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:26.700
His name was Guy, funnily enough.

00:12:26.766 --> 00:12:28.166
We met at a dinner party.

00:12:28.166 --> 00:12:29.066
He heard I was a singer.

00:12:29.066 --> 00:12:30.566
He said, "Do you have an album?"

00:12:30.566 --> 00:12:33.233
I said, "No, but I have an idea."

00:12:33.233 --> 00:12:36.366
And while I was speaking,
he reached into his pocket,

00:12:36.366 --> 00:12:40.400
pulled out his wallet,
and said, "I'll take the first copy."

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.266
And I kind of looked at him
and I looked at the money,

00:12:42.266 --> 00:12:45.600
and then I asked the obvious
question, "Guy, why are you supporting me?

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.600
You haven't heard me sing."

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:50.700
He said, "Verity,
isn't that the point of your project?" Yes.

00:12:50.766 --> 00:12:55.333
Give me that money.

00:12:55.433 --> 00:12:57.333
But in that moment, Guy

00:12:57.333 --> 00:13:00.300
taught me a life changing lesson.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:03.800
And that is that your word creates
your world.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.833
The way you speak about your goal,
your dream, where you're going,

00:13:06.833 --> 00:13:11.066
that's what determines
who shows up to help you get there.

00:13:11.133 --> 00:13:14.866
And the more I told people
I was selling an album that didn't exist,

00:13:14.966 --> 00:13:17.033
the more I sold it.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.666
So very long story short,

00:13:18.666 --> 00:13:23.266
over 2000 people in 25 countries
bought that album.

00:13:23.366 --> 00:13:25.133
I released it in 2008.

00:13:25.133 --> 00:13:27.966
They sent me pictures, poems, postcards.

00:13:27.966 --> 00:13:30.433
I even got a proposal
from a guy in Edinburgh,

00:13:30.433 --> 00:13:32.866
but I think he wanted
to make more than an album,

00:13:32.866 --> 00:13:36.600
so I said no.

00:13:36.666 --> 00:13:40.666
As a result of them saying yes to my idea
and to my dream,

00:13:40.766 --> 00:13:44.400
we were able to give over $4,000
to charity.

00:13:44.466 --> 00:13:46.533
I could buy supplies for the shelters.

00:13:46.533 --> 00:13:49.200
I could pay for two singers
to record their demos,

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:52.700
and one of them, Mimi Ntenjwa, pictured
there in the middle,

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:57.933
went on in 2010 to be named
Best Young Jazz Artist in South Africa.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:03.000
Isn't that amazing?

00:14:03.066 --> 00:14:05.300
So I'm just going to address
the elephant in the room.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:09.566
You do know that I'm
not a famous pop star.

00:14:09.633 --> 00:14:11.733
I'm still open to that interview
with Oprah,

00:14:11.733 --> 00:14:13.600
just saying.

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:16.100
But what happened with this project

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:19.900
was that I got better known
for thinking than I did for singing.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.566
I was suddenly being asked to speak
all over the world and I'd say, "Wow,

00:14:23.566 --> 00:14:24.633
can I bring my band?"

00:14:24.633 --> 00:14:27.366
And they'd say, "No.

00:14:27.366 --> 00:14:30.300
Come teach us
how to think ahead of the crowd."

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.300
Because ultimately, that's
what people were interested in.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:36.500
They were documentaries
and news articles worldwide.

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:40.133
It was one of the first online
crowdfunding projects.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:42.666
HSBC Bank in the UK put it at number

00:14:42.666 --> 00:14:48.066
25 of their top 100 business ideas,
and Cape Town singer Verity

00:14:48.066 --> 00:14:53.333
even appeared in "Carte Blanche,"
the James Bond novel by Jeffery Deaver.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.400
I don't know if you know what that means.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:14:57.900
I'm officially South Africa's first

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:04.033
Bond girl.

00:15:04.133 --> 00:15:07.133
But the most unexpected
thing that happened

00:15:07.166 --> 00:15:10.900
was when I got sent a picture of Edward

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:16.233
de Bono, the author of "Six Thinking
Hats," holding a copy of my album,

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:19.133
holding proof that I'd acted on the idea.

00:15:19.133 --> 00:15:20.600
Kay and I met him on his last

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:24.600
ever visit to South Africa,
and he invited us to teach his work,

00:15:24.666 --> 00:15:26.966
and it changed my life.

00:15:26.966 --> 00:15:30.933
Now, imagine
if I'd never acted on that idea.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.966
None of this would have happened.

00:15:33.966 --> 00:15:37.466
Now, it's not the path
my soul or my ego wanted for me.

00:15:37.466 --> 00:15:38.700
I wanted to be a pop star,

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:41.066
but what I've learned is that sometimes

00:15:41.066 --> 00:15:45.333
when you get onto the road
that your soul has planned for you,

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:48.066
life gets so much easier.

00:15:48.066 --> 00:15:51.633
So I want to circle back to your road,

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:55.266
to that dream
that you put your hand up about earlier.

00:15:55.333 --> 00:15:58.933
And I think you'll agree with me
that when it comes to chasing your dreams,

00:15:58.933 --> 00:16:02.333
if you don't design your future,
someone or

00:16:02.333 --> 00:16:05.333
something else
is going to design it for you.

00:16:05.366 --> 00:16:09.033
And do you want to design
your future? Here, at home,

00:16:09.033 --> 00:16:10.766
I hope at home it's also saying yes.

00:16:10.766 --> 00:16:12.733
Do you want to design your future?

00:16:12.733 --> 00:16:13.966
Yes. Okay.

00:16:13.966 --> 00:16:16.633
Let's look at how you do
that. Here's the framework.

00:16:16.633 --> 00:16:18.300
Hold that dream in your heart

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:22.533
and now I want you to look to the horizon
of that dream and get very clear

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:25.300
on what is your desired outcome.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:28.300
You need to frame this
as an extraordinary question.

00:16:28.333 --> 00:16:31.533
"How can I?" and then put some stretch in it.

00:16:31.533 --> 00:16:34.333
This question needs to make your brain
a little bit uncomfortable

00:16:34.333 --> 00:16:36.300
so that it can get creative.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.366
When you've got that focus,

00:16:38.366 --> 00:16:42.666
step back and look at the
facts of your current reality.

00:16:42.733 --> 00:16:43.800
And then

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.800
take some time to check in
with how you're feeling.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.500
But please remember, name the feelings.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:52.266
Don't explain the feelings.

00:16:52.266 --> 00:16:54.133
You're getting this.

00:16:54.133 --> 00:16:56.333
And then I want to ask you
to spend a lot of time.

00:16:56.333 --> 00:16:57.633
Don't wait two years to look

00:16:57.633 --> 00:17:00.700
for the positives of what's happening
with regards to your dream.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.533
Look for the positives,
what's working, what's going for you?

00:17:03.533 --> 00:17:06.766
How can you leverage that
to take you forward?

00:17:06.866 --> 00:17:09.366
And then get very honest
about what's in the way?

00:17:09.366 --> 00:17:14.166
What are the weaknesses,
the pitfalls, the potholes, the problems?

00:17:14.233 --> 00:17:18.333
And then I want you to fill
those potholes in with your ideas.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.400
It helps to invite the Kay in your life
to help you with this.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:24.333
I find having a thinking
partner makes a big difference.

00:17:24.333 --> 00:17:27.700
But this is where you need
to have wild ideas that you can tame

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:28.966
rather than boring ideas

00:17:28.966 --> 00:17:33.433
that you try to make interesting
because when you have that idea

00:17:33.533 --> 00:17:37.333
and it excites you, it delights you,
it ignites a passion in you,

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.900
that's when you have to act.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:42.566
That's when you have to dare
to flirt with failure.

00:17:42.566 --> 00:17:47.300
That's when you have to let your word
create your world.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.766
And I'm not going to tell you it's easy

00:17:49.766 --> 00:17:53.366
because it's not. Dreams of things we do
when we're awake,

00:17:53.366 --> 00:17:56.366
not when we're asleep,
but when you step out your comfort zone

00:17:56.466 --> 00:18:01.533
and you see where it can take you,
it can be a life changing journey.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:05.200
So is that a journey that you want to go
on to make your dream a reality?

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:06.700
Yes, yes. Yes, yes.

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:06.966
I see

00:18:06.966 --> 00:18:09.966
Lisa Nichols is going to be with
us all weekend.

00:18:10.133 --> 00:18:12.300
Yes, yes.

00:18:12.366 --> 00:18:14.866
So I want
you to really take this forward with you

00:18:14.866 --> 00:18:19.766
is that your thinking that you do today
is affecting the results you get tomorrow

00:18:19.833 --> 00:18:22.400
and every day you have the choice
to look at the problems

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:25.966
you're facing
and think about them differently.

00:18:26.066 --> 00:18:31.066
So my Toastmasters here, my Toastmasters
at home, what I want to ask you,

00:18:31.133 --> 00:18:35.900
and I just want to get the buy in from you
that you are going to do this.

00:18:36.033 --> 00:18:39.033
Let's see, I'm switching
mics. We're going to get interesting here.

00:18:39.266 --> 00:18:42.233
Are you ready to design your future?

00:18:42.233 --> 00:18:45.233
Are you ready to think ahead of the crowd?

00:18:45.266 --> 00:18:50.033
Are you ready to get onto the road
that your soul has planned for you?

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:54.300
Well, to help you on that journey,
I'd like to leave you with a soundtrack,

00:18:54.533 --> 00:18:57.233
something that you can sing
while you start walking.

00:18:57.233 --> 00:18:59.700
And I've lost them. They're going
to play it for me very nicely.

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:02.900
I've got amazing,
my band is at the back of the room.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:03.866
They're going to give us,

00:19:03.866 --> 00:19:04.800
they are going to give us a song.

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:07.700
Would you like a song?
Can I finish with a song?

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:12.400
This is my invitation to chase your dreams
because you'd be crazy

00:19:12.566 --> 00:19:18.100
not to try.

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:22.233
Maybe I'm crazy thinking that this might

00:19:22.233 --> 00:19:24.900
work. Maybe I'm crazy

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:28.366
wanting something that might hurt.

00:19:28.466 --> 00:19:33.333
Maybe it's madness to start
something that might end.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:36.300
Maybe it's easier

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:38.266
to just not begin.

00:19:38.266 --> 00:19:40.666
I may be delusional

00:19:40.666 --> 00:19:43.666
believing this stands a chance

00:19:43.833 --> 00:19:48.733
when so many before have proved
that good things don't last.

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:53.900
Seems a little bit
naive to step in anyway,

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:57.933
but I'd be crazy if I didn't

00:19:57.933 --> 00:20:02.533
take a step around the corner
confronting all that

00:20:02.533 --> 00:20:04.066
I don't know.

00:20:04.066 --> 00:20:08.700
The storms, our weather,
those times of being out of control.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:14.166
Not having all the answers
is never a reason not to try.

00:20:14.233 --> 00:20:26.800
What if the Wright brothers
hadn't believed that they could fly?

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:36.666
I believe that we can fly.

00:20:36.733 --> 00:20:37.833
You'd be crazy

00:20:37.833 --> 00:20:39.700
not to try.

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:41.200
I hope you get out there.

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:42.933
I hope you chase your dreams.

00:20:42.933 --> 00:20:45.700
You give yourself permission
to think ahead of the crowd,

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:50.666
and when you get there, please
send me a poem, a postcard, and a picture.

00:20:50.733 --> 00:20:52.433
Thank you, Toastmasters.

